WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:07.780
My name is Becca Krouse.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.820
I am a data scientist at GSK in
our statistics and data science

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.830
innovation hub, where my team
is working on building support

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.430
systems and facilitating
the adoption of R

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.620
across biostatistics.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.895
Today I'll be talking about R
package assessment lessons that

00:00:26.895 --> 00:00:28.020
can be learned from pharma.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.890
In today's world,
we have no shortage

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.230
of products right
at our fingertips

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.980
that can be received in
just days or even hours

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.780
with the click of a mouse.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.600
Vendors of all shapes and
sizes have the ability

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.710
to reach consumers
across the globe, which

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.500
means as consumers,
we have a wide variety

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.013
of features to choose from, but
also quite a range of quality.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.080
Sound familiar?

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.090
Whether you're
looking for something

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.032
you need on Amazon or
a package on CRAN,

00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:08.490
you probably want
to find something

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:12.020
that suits your needs
that you can count on.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.950
So what?

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.310
How do you make those decisions?

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.440
What do you look for?

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.300
You're probably going
to look at indicators

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.710
that you feel are important.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:25.370
So if you think about how
you approach online shopping,

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:29.480
you probably go to the
reviews, the ratings, badges,

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.130
the name of the seller.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.320
And depending on what
you're looking for,

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.060
how much time you
have, maybe you

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.890
deep dive into those reviews.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.460
While the indicators, and
I'll say the indicators

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.680
give you an overall
picture of confidence

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.970
of whether the product is
going to look, feel, and perform

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.200
as advertised.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.760
While the indicators
are a little bit

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.070
different for packages, the
overall idea is the same.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.660
A little bit of
research is going

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.290
to go a long way to build
that picture of confidence

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:06.880
that the package is going
to produce results that you

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.250
can count on that are accurate.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:16.750
Now, this idea is absolutely
critical to those of us in

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.250
I mean, it doesn't
matter your work,

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.350
but it's especially
critical to those of us

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.320
in regulated
industries like pharma,

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.090
where it's really
important for us

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.660
to deliver safe and
effective drugs to patients

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.170
as quickly as we can.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:37.720
And to do so, we need to make
sure our data and our analysis,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:44.750
our entire pipelines are free
of errors and full of integrity.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.230
A huge part off of our
pipelines is the software

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.800
that's used to produce the
results, transform the data,

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.390
all that type of stuff.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.800
We we really want
to do our research,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.570
and the regulators expect
that we do our due diligence

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.930
and make defensible choices.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.500
So regulators expect that
our choices are defensible,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.190
meaning that we want
to do our due diligence

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:14.000
and provide sufficient
documentation of why we feel

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.690
that our software, our
packages, work correctly

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:21.210
and produce correct results.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.140
So let's think about
that for a minute.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.520
How do we really know
our results are correct?

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.980
Well, one thing we
can do is compare

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.810
against a source of truth.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.470
That source of truth could
be your common sense,

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.660
another programmer, another
programming language,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:43.330
maybe a publication
like a textbook, even.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:47.260
And this is true
regardless of whether using

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.230
the latest and greatest R
package or a SAS proc that's

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.840
been around for decades.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.760
It's all the same
idea, and we want

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.710
to make sure that our results
are going to be accurate

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.790
for many scenarios we
haven't encountered yet.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:01.962
Many new data sets,

00:04:01.962 --> 00:04:03.045
new studies in the future.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.220
We're in exciting
times now and pharma

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.970
is making progress full speed
ahead towards R

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:17.130
Being a primary tool used in
studies and submission work.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.070
Folks across the industry are
putting their heads together

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:25.020
to align on a common
philosophy for what

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.560
it takes to ensure
that our R packages are

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.270
accurate. In the R validation hub,

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:37.710
specifically, this philosophy
focuses on a package assessment,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.930
and there's some principles
and some ideas in place,

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.540
but it's up to the
individual organizations

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.390
to then implement
it for themselves

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.080
and really decide how
much evidence is enough.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.420
At GSK, where we're working
hard on our own implementation

00:04:56.420 --> 00:05:02.450
and we've settled on a set of
guiding principles for how we

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.610
think about package assessment.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.290
And I'll present
those to you today

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:09.250
as the domains of
trustworthiness.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.110
There's five of them.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:15.250
Each one plays an important
role in the overall assessment.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.900
And each one consists of
a collection of things.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:25.000
Those things are sometimes quite
easy to measure, quantifiable.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.690
But others are a little bit
more nuanced, harder to measure,

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:30.640
more subjective.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.860
So let's step through these now.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.420
Beginning with the
Community domain.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.590
The purpose of this domain is to
get an idea of how broadly used

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:47.050
a package is in the community,
how much folks are enjoying it,

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:50.510
how strong its roots are.

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:53.090
So specifically, we can look
at something like downloads

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:57.270
to get an idea of the
volume of use to date.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.780
And that volume of
use is an indicator

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.490
of how much the
package has been vetted

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:07.000
in real life scenarios, how
much it's been stress tested.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.480
We are also interested in the
overall trajectory of that use.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.260
Is it gaining traction?

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:16.010
Is the package maintaining
or declining for some reason?

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.010
Maybe there's another
package that's come along

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:21.880
that's superseded it.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:25.480
We also want to look at
how the community relates

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:28.820
to the package and
its maintainers.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.130
Both how the community
relies on this package,

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:34.310
depends on this package
as part of other software,

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:37.960
or other materials like books.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.610
And also going the
opposite direction,

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:45.220
How much the package maintainer
has a vested interest

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:48.910
in supporting the
community and keeping up

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:55.150
with the ever-evolving many
moving parts of the community.

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:59.200
Now something to keep in mind
is in an industry like pharma,

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.670
we have some fairly
specific needs.

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:04.000
Maybe our packages
that we are choosing

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.630
are a little more niche, or
we have some internal packages

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:08.570
that we're using.

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:12.430
So by nature these are going
to have a little bit smaller

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:13.850
community user base,

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:14.810
and that's OK.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:16.830
We have other domains
to look at as well.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.980
With the Authorship domain.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:27.380
We are doing some research to
learn who the developers are,

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:31.160
what are their backgrounds,
their experience,

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:34.220
and really does that experience,
is that an appropriate match

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.680
for the complexity of the tool?

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.410
We found this one to
be fairly important

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:43.220
for statistical packages
where that level of expertise

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:47.390
is quite high, quite specific.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:50.600
So some things
that we look at here

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:55.680
are the overall reputation, how the
community regards this author

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.080
as an expert in their
field, as an R developer,

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.800
and how much experience they
have in both those areas.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.410
So something you can look
at is the other packages

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:10.360
that that person or those individuals
might have out there.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:15.520
And any specific qualifications,
degrees, certificates,

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.310
things like that, all
round out our picture

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:22.180
of who these authors are and
what their backgrounds are.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:25.330
And this domain is
quite dependent on what

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.850
you can find out there,
which obviously varies

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:29.360
from individual to individual.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.200
So, it's important nonetheless.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.190
Our next domain is the
Documentation domain,

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:42.090
which here I intend to cover

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.520
functionality documentation.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.390
So what is the tool
supposed to do?

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.740
What is the intent, and

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:51.670
What is the scope?

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:55.230
What are the limits of that
tool to help us understand

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:56.650
what is appropriate use?

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.740
What is responsible use?

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:03.060
This is kind of a prerequisite
to being able to prove

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:03.670
accuracy.

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:06.570
You need to understand
in which cases your function

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:10.600
is going to produce
correct results.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.240
So documentation
we are all probably

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:14.950
pretty familiar
with looking around

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:18.700
at help files,
vignettes, examples.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:23.150
So what we're after is,
do those things exist?

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:25.510
But also what is their quality?

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:27.520
How helpful and clear are they?

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:29.930
What types of
scenarios are covered?

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:34.210
And then for the intended
use, clear documentation

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:35.840
of expected inputs.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:38.140
Expected outputs.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:39.110
Really nice.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:43.960
If edge case, the limits
piece is covered in there.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:44.950
What what

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:48.520
What might we want to
watch out for when we're

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:52.300
using this tool, for instance?

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:57.730
And this leads us into the
Software Development Life Cycle

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:02.010
domain, which we are
aiming to capture

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:06.970
the overall process for how
the code develops and evolves

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:13.790
over time, how it is
cared for, essentially.

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:15.290
With this one,

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:17.990
first and foremost,
we need transparency

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:20.030
into what's happening.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:25.760
And luckily for us, this is
increasingly easy with things

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:29.160
like GitHub, which is
pretty popular these days,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.330
it allows us to get a
peek into what's going on,

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:34.430
what kind of branching
model is being

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:37.280
used, how are things being
worked on and merged in,

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.380
and are there any

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.370
What is the roadmap of the tool?

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:45.050
Are there any
critical open issues?

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:47.780
All things we can look
at just by peeking

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.270
into that repository.

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:54.110
And to get a sense
of how well supported

00:10:54.110 --> 00:11:00.710
it is, we can also look at the
activity, how quickly the code

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.660
or how often the code
is being worked on.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:05.190
Issues being closed.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:08.290
Releases to CRAN.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:10.780
And then any safeguards
that are in place

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:15.130
to protect the integrity
of the code as it evolves.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:18.280
Protected branches,
continuous checking,

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:20.590
continuous testing,
all to prevent

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:27.020
against any bugs being
introduced as this changes.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:29.450
Now something to keep
in mind is a package

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:33.110
could have all the hallmarks
of a good package here.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:35.750
But maybe it's
really, really new

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:39.510
and it hasn't had a chance
to work out some of the bugs.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:42.840
And, you know, we're
not aware of those yet.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:44.990
And it hasn't been
stress tested enough yet

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:47.420
or it's changing a lot.

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:49.520
On the other end of
things, a package

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:51.560
could have fairly
stalled development,

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.020
which could mean
it's been abandoned

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:56.070
or it could mean it's just
really, really stable.

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:59.060
So something to keep in mind
and looking at this domain

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:02.610
is what is the history
of the project?

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.070
Where is it in terms
of its lifecycle?

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:10.490
And our final domain
is the Testing domain.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:13.370
So here, testing, I
mean, the actual testing

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:17.000
inside the package,
oftentimes this is your test

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.010
that your unit tests.

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:21.890
And what these do is check
that the functionality

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:27.780
is working as expected and that
it's producing correct results.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:32.070
Which sounds a lot like
our definition of accuracy

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:33.810
that we talked about earlier.

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:38.130
So a really solid test suite can
be essentially a gold standard

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:41.340
for how you can prove accuracy.

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:44.520
So let's take a look
at what we might

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.730
look for inside the package.

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:50.460
So this is your
package directory,

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:53.280
but we can look at
whether a test exists

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:54.790
and what they're covering.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.670
So things like code
coverage, which

00:12:56.670 --> 00:13:01.380
which functions are being
covered and to what extent.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.750
And from there, we
can look specifically

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:05.430
at what we're
interested in, what are

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:07.840
the key things that we feel

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:10.370
we are going to
be using the most?

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:13.290
And how deep that
testing is on those.

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.430
So what types of scenarios
are covered there?

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:20.930
What types of inputs
are the developers

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:25.670
looking for and are they
comparing against some known

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:26.670
value?

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:29.030
Even better if that
source of truth

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:33.360
comes from some
outside resource.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.780
Now I recognize this is
a fairly technical task,

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:39.150
so this may look a little
bit different depending

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.850
on who you are and what
resources you have.

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:44.790
But even being familiar
with the first two,

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:47.610
the presence, the depth, sorry,
the presence, the breath,

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.370
and then taking a
peek into the depth,

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:53.430
maybe like some of those
descriptions in the test, that

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:55.890
can be very helpful.

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:59.520
Another thing to keep in
mind about the testing domain

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:03.030
is not everything is
super easy to test.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:06.870
Sometimes visualizations
or complex algorithms

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.790
can be a little
bit--really novel

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:13.300
complex algorithms can be a
little bit tougher to test.

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:15.110
So that's OK.

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:20.560
It's just, you know, again, we
have the rest of the domains.

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:25.110
So, with those in mind.

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:28.740
The assessment may look a
little bit different depending

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:32.790
on who you are and what you do.

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:38.160
For us, we are very much working
towards R for submission

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:41.740
work. We're very risk
averse in this situation.

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:47.550
We do a pretty
in-depth assessment.

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:51.510
So if you're just kind of
poking around exploring,

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.560
maybe you do a little bit
lighter of assessment.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.960
But getting to know your
packages is really important

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.085
and it will serve you well.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.570
Also on a task level, if you're

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:08.720
transforming the data,
changing the data in some way

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:12.480
or performing a
statistical analysis,

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:18.600
the stakes might be a little bit
higher if something goes awry.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:20.290
As compared to.

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:22.410
And also it might be harder
to tell when something

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.630
goes awry as compared
to something that's

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:27.000
more aesthetic in nature.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:30.180
So again, your approach
to the assessment

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:31.925
might change depending on that.

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.290
And so you could also
perform this whole assessment

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:44.140
and not be quite satisfied
with the results.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:47.130
But maybe you have
only one option

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:49.260
and this is the
package you need.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:52.530
So maybe you're willing to
bend a little bit on your risk

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:55.360
and build some extra safeguards
around that for yourself.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:58.100
Augment it with some
testing or extra QC.

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:07.410
So even if you find yourself
in the left side very much

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:12.120
in the stable, risk averse,
the rest of the ecosystem

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:14.590
is going to continue
to evolve and change.

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:16.680
And that's the
beauty of R, right?

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:21.180
There's cutting edge, newer
things every single day.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:24.570
So it's important for us,
if we are in that situation,

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:29.390
to keep our eyes up ahead of
what's to come and find ways

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:32.480
to innovate, find
ways to experiment

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:37.640
and keep our minds open for
any gems that come along.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:41.090
And we can in turn then grow
our tried and true collection

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:43.050
of packages that we count on.

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:48.770
All right.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:50.920
So I said there
were five domains,

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:53.470
but I'm going to throw
you one more, which

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:57.160
is the domain about
thinking holistically.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:00.220
So when we do these
assessments, we

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:06.099
are not expecting a
perfect score by any means.

00:17:06.099 --> 00:17:09.069
The packages--the
purpose of the assessment

00:17:09.069 --> 00:17:12.369
is to allow these packages to
shine where they are strong

00:17:12.369 --> 00:17:15.250
and let those strengths
or weaknesses guide us

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:17.980
in the rest of our
assessment and help

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:21.369
us focus in on what matters.

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:25.420
So for instance, a package
could have really strong tests,

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:29.660
but maybe not as much community
support or vice versa.

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:34.090
So these things can kind of
counter each other, right?

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:38.350
So if we're flexible and
adaptable in our approach,

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:43.870
then we can more effectively
collect that body of evidence

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:46.510
that's going to inform
our sense of trust

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:50.820
in this wonderfully
imperfect open source world.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:52.730
Thank you.

